const V7_PACK={"version":7,"revision":"combined-worked-guide-v7","margins":0.3,"minimumBodyPt":6.1,"sheets":[[["notation","summary","percentile"],["discrete","discrete-cdf","loaded","events"],["density","piecewise"]],[["endpoint","uniform"],["normal","quantile","continuous-graph"],["sampling","linear","precision","binary","r-normal"]],[["one-t","pooled-t","t-output"],["anova","bonferroni"],["bonferroni-pairs","holm-t","holm","tables","anova-output"]],[["z-yates","dioxin","gof"],["chi2","large-chi"],["risk","folate-risk","odds","design","chi-output"]]],"blocks":{"notation":{"title":"Data types and notation","html":"<p><b>BME423 · V7.</b> Population: the entire group; sample: the measured subset. A population parameter is fixed; a sample statistic changes across samples. Keep full calculator precision between steps.</p><p>\\(x_i\\) (observation \\(i\\)); \\(n\\) (sample size); \\(\\bar x\\) (sample mean); \\(\\mu\\) (population mean); \\(s\\) (sample SD); \\(\\sigma\\) (population SD); \\(s^2,\\sigma^2\\) (variances). \\(\\sum\\) means add the specified terms.</p><p><b>Interval–continuous:</b> measurements on a continuum, e.g. blood pressure, height or membrane potential. <b>Interval–discrete:</b> counts or rounded measurements, e.g. tender joints, text-message count or age rounded to years. <b>Categorical–nominal:</b> unordered categories, e.g. country or ZIP code. <b>Categorical–ordered:</b> categories with a natural order, e.g. cancer stage or severity. A numeric label such as ZIP code is not an interval measurement.</p><p>\\(\\nu\\) (degrees of freedom); \\(\\alpha\\) (test significance level); \\(f\\) (mass/density); \\(F\\) (CDF).</p>","sources":["session2.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["data-scales","notation"],"page":1,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: additional symbol meanings"]},"summary":{"title":"Height data: mean, variance, SD and SEM","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Heights (inches): 65, 69, 59, 64, 62, 67, 68, 62, 64, 65. Estimate the mean, variance, SD and standard error of the mean.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Add the observations; divide by their count</h4>\\[\\bar x=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i}{n}\\]\\[n=10,\\quad \\sum x_i=645\\]\\[\\bar x=\\frac{645}{10}=64.5\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Subtract the mean, then square each difference</h4>\\[d_i=x_i-\\bar x,\\qquad d_i^2=(x_i-\\bar x)^2\\]\\[d_1=65-64.5=.5,\\quad d_1^2=.5^2=.25\\]\\[d_2=69-64.5=4.5,\\quad d_2^2=4.5^2=20.25\\]\\[d_3=59-64.5=-5.5,\\quad d_3^2=(-5.5)^2=30.25\\]\\[d_4=64-64.5=-.5,\\quad d_4^2=(-.5)^2=.25\\]\\[d_5=62-64.5=-2.5,\\quad d_5^2=(-2.5)^2=6.25\\]\\[d_6=67-64.5=2.5,\\quad d_6^2=2.5^2=6.25\\]\\[d_7=68-64.5=3.5,\\quad d_7^2=3.5^2=12.25\\]\\[d_8=62-64.5=-2.5,\\quad d_8^2=(-2.5)^2=6.25\\]\\[d_9=64-64.5=-.5,\\quad d_9^2=(-.5)^2=.25\\]\\[d_{10}=65-64.5=.5,\\quad d_{10}^2=.5^2=.25\\]\\[\\sum d_i^2=82.5\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Divide by one less than the sample size</h4>\\[s^2=\\frac{\\sum (x_i-\\bar x)^2}{n-1}\\]\\[s^2=\\frac{82.5}{10-1}=9.16667\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Square-root variance; scale SD for a mean</h4>\\[s=\\sqrt{s^2},\\qquad SEM=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}\\]\\[s=\\sqrt{9.16667}=3.02765\\]\\[SEM=\\frac{3.02765}{\\sqrt{10}}=.957427\\]<p>SD describes variation among heights; SEM describes variation among repeated sample means. Variance has squared units; SD and SEM have the original units.</p></div><p><b>Whole population instead:</b> use its population mean and divide squared deviations by \\(N\\), not \\(n-1\\).</p>\\[\\mu=\\frac{\\sum_i x_i}{N},\\qquad\\sigma^2=\\frac{\\sum_i(x_i-\\mu)^2}{N}\\]<div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Squared-sum check for the same variance numerator</h4>\\[\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2=\\sum_i x_i^2-\\frac{(\\sum_i x_i)^2}{n}\\]\\[\\sum_i x_i^2=41685,\\qquad (\\sum_i x_i)^2=645^2=416025\\]\\[41685-\\frac{416025}{10}=41685-41602.5=82.5\\]<p>The same 82.5 is divided by 9 for the sample variance.</p></div><p><b>Estimators:</b> \\(\\bar x\\) estimates \\(\\mu\\); \\(s\\) estimates \\(\\sigma\\). Unbiased means correctly centered across repeated samples. For a Normal population, the sample mean has the minimum-variance property discussed in class.</p>\\[\\widehat{SE}(s^2)=s^2\\sqrt{\\frac2{n-1}}=9.16667\\sqrt{\\frac29}=4.32121\\]<p>This estimates uncertainty in the sample variance for Normal observations; it is different from SEM.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session2.pdf"],"topics":["mean","variance","sd","sem"],"page":1,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: squared-sum shortcut and intermediate arithmetic","V5: estimator properties, uncertainty in variance, evaluated for the same height sample"]},"percentile":{"title":"Sorted heights: median, quartiles and IQR","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> For the same heights, find the median, quartiles, IQR and range. Sorted data: 59, 62, 62, 64, 64, 65, 65, 67, 68, 69.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Locate a percentile between sorted ranks</h4>\\[R=1+\\frac{p(n-1)}{100}=I+d\\]\\[R_{25}=1+\\frac{25(10-1)}{100}=3.25\\]\\[R_{50}=1+\\frac{50(10-1)}{100}=5.5\\]\\[R_{75}=1+\\frac{75(10-1)}{100}=7.75\\]<p>\\(p\\) (percentile number); \\(I\\) (integer rank); \\(d\\) (fraction of the gap to the next rank).</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Interpolate the measured values, not the ranks</h4>\\[x_p=x_{(I)}+d[x_{(I+1)}-x_{(I)}]\\]\\[Q_1=62+.25(64-62)=62.5\\]\\[Q_2=64+.5(65-64)=64.5\\]\\[Q_3=65+.75(67-65)=66.5\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. IQR and observed range (59–69)</h4>\\[IQR=Q_3-Q_1,\\quad \\text{range}=x_{\\max}-x_{\\min}\\]\\[IQR=66.5-62.5=4\\]\\[\\text{range}=69-59=10\\]</div><p><b>Shape:</b> a roughly straight Normal Q–Q plot supports a Normal shape. A box spans Q1–Q3 and marks the median; the usual whisker cutoff is 1.5×IQR. Histogram height represents bin counts.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session2.pdf"],"topics":["percentiles","median","iqr"],"page":1,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: graphical summaries and Normal shape"]},"discrete":{"title":"Discrete table: probabilities, mean and variance","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Check this probability function. Find its mean, variance, CDF, and the probability that the outcome is at least 2 but less than 7.</p><table><thead><tr><th>\\(x\\)</th><th>-1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\\(p(x)\\)</td><td>.55</td><td>.15</td><td>.10</td><td>.20</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Check both probability requirements</h4>\\[0\\le p(x)\\le1,\\qquad \\sum_xp(x)=1\\]\\[.55+.15+.10+.20=1\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Weight each value by its probability</h4>\\[\\mu=E[X]=\\sum_x x\\,p(x)\\]\\[\\begin{aligned}\\mu&=(-1)(.55)+2(.15)\\\\&\\quad+3(.10)+7(.20)\\\\&=-.55+.30+.30+1.40=1.45\\end{aligned}\\]<p>\\(X\\) (random variable before observing the outcome); \\(x\\) (one possible value); \\(p(x)\\) (its probability).</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Square the values before weighting</h4>\\[E[X^2]=\\sum_x x^2p(x)\\]\\[\\begin{aligned}E[X^2]&=1(.55)+4(.15)\\\\&\\quad+9(.10)+49(.20)\\\\&=.55+.60+.90+9.80=11.85\\end{aligned}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Direct variance: weight EVERY squared deviation</h4>\\[v_x=(x-\\mu)^2p(x),\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(X)=\\sum_x v_x\\]\\[v_{-1}=(-1-1.45)^2(.55)=3.30138\\]\\[v_2=(2-1.45)^2(.15)=.045375\\]\\[v_3=(3-1.45)^2(.10)=.24025\\]\\[v_7=(7-1.45)^2(.20)=6.1605\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=3.30138+.045375+.24025+6.1605\\]\\[=9.7475\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. Check with the second-moment shortcut</h4>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=11.85-1.45^2=9.7475\\]\\[SD(X)=\\sqrt{9.7475}=3.1221\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>6. Add only the included masses</h4>\\[P(X\\in A)=\\sum_{x\\in A}p(x)\\]\\[P(2\\le X<7)=p(2)+p(3)=.15+.10=.25\\]</div>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session3.pdf"],"topics":["pmf","expectation","variance-discrete"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"discrete-cdf":{"title":"Discrete CDF and graph endpoints","html":"<div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Accumulate probabilities through the endpoint</h4>\\[F(x)=P(X\\le x)=\\sum_{t\\le x}p(t)\\]\\[F(x)=\\begin{cases}0&x<-1\\\\.55&-1\\le x<2\\\\.70&2\\le x<3\\\\.80&3\\le x<7\\\\1&x\\ge7\\end{cases}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Include or exclude the endpoint mass</h4>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a)\\]\\[P(X>2)=1-.70=.30\\]\\[P(X\\ge2)=1-P(X<2)=1-.55=.45\\]\\[P(-1<X<3)=p(2)=.15\\]</div><img class=\"v7-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-discrete.svg\" alt=\"Discrete mass plot and right-continuous step CDF\"><p><b>Sketch:</b> PMF uses separate mass spikes. CDF jumps by each mass, stays flat between values, and includes the new height at the jump (filled dot); the lower endpoint is open. A discrete point can have positive probability.</p>","sources":["session3.pdf","hw1.pdf"],"topics":["cdf-discrete","graph-discrete"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"loaded":{"title":"Loaded die: use the stated probabilities","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> A die has \\(p(1)=.20\\); each of 2–6 has probability .16. Find \\(P(X\\le3)\\), mean and variance.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Weight using the stated masses</h4>\\[P(X\\le a)=\\sum_{x\\le a}p(x),\\quad E[X]=\\sum xp(x)\\]\\[P(X\\le3)=.20+.16+.16=.52\\]\\[E[X]=1(.20)+(2+3+4+5+6)(.16)=3.4\\]\\[E[X^2]=1(.20)+(4+9+16+25+36)(.16)=14.6\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\mu^2=14.6-3.4^2=3.04\\]</div>","sources":["session3.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["loaded-die"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"events":{"title":"Successive events: update the available outcomes","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Two cards are drawn without replacement from 52 cards. Find the probability that both are kings.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Without replacement: update the second draw</h4>\\[P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B\\mid A)\\]\\[P(\\text{two kings})=\\frac4{52}\\frac3{51}=.00452489\\]<p>For independent events, the second probability stays unchanged. Three fair coin flips have 8 equally likely sequences; exactly two heads occurs as HHT, HTH or THH, so its probability is 3/8.</p></div>","sources":["session3.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["conditional-probability"],"page":1,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"density":{"title":"Continuous density: integrate, then evaluate bounds","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> For \\(f(x)=x/2\\) on (0,2), zero elsewhere, find the total area, CDF, mean, variance, a tail and median.</p><p><b>Integration rule:</b> \\(dx\\) names the integration variable. Raise its power by one, divide by that new power; evaluate upper bound minus lower bound.</p>\\[\\int_a^b kx^r\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{kx^{r+1}}{r+1}\\right]_a^b\\quad(r\\ne-1)\\]<div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Check total area and nonnegative height</h4>\\[\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f(x)\\,dx=1\\]\\[\\int_0^2\\frac{x}{2}\\,dx=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^{1+1}}{1+1}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\frac{2^2}{4}-\\frac{0^2}{4}=1\\]<p>The density is nonnegative on its support. Density height is not probability; probability is area. \\(\\int_a^b g(x)\\,dx=G(b)-G(a)\\) means evaluate the antiderivative at the upper bound, then subtract its lower-bound value.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Accumulate area only up to the requested value</h4>\\[F(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}f(t)\\,dt\\]\\[F(x)=\\int_0^x\\frac t2\\,dt=\\left[\\frac{t^2}{4}\\right]_0^x=\\frac{x^2}{4}\\]\\[F(x)=\\begin{cases}0&x\\le0\\\\x^2/4&0<x<2\\\\1&x\\ge2\\end{cases}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Multiply by the outcome before integrating</h4>\\[\\mu=E[X]=\\int x f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[E[X]=\\int_0^2 x\\frac{x}{2}\\,dx=\\int_0^2\\frac{x^2}{2}\\,dx\\]\\[=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^{2+1}}{2+1}\\right]_0^2=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{6}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\frac{2^3}{6}-\\frac{0^3}{6}=\\frac86=\\frac43\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Use a second moment for variance</h4>\\[E[X^2]=\\int x^2f(x)\\,dx,\\quad \\sigma^2=E[X^2]-\\mu^2\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\frac12\\int_0^2x^3\\,dx=\\frac12\\left[\\frac{x^{3+1}}{3+1}\\right]_0^2\\]\\[=\\left[\\frac{x^4}{8}\\right]_0^2=\\frac{2^4}{8}-\\frac{0^4}{8}=2\\]\\[\\sigma^2=2-\\left(\\frac43\\right)^2=\\frac{18-16}{9}=\\frac29\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. A right tail is the unaccumulated area</h4>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a)\\]\\[F(1.5)=\\frac{1.5^2}{4}=.5625\\]\\[P(X>1.5)=1-.5625=.4375\\]\\[=\\int_{1.5}^2\\frac x2\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{4}\\right]_{1.5}^2=\\frac44-\\frac{2.25}{4}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>6. A median splits the total area in half</h4>\\[F(m)=.5\\]\\[\\frac{m^2}{4}=.5\\Rightarrow m^2=2\\Rightarrow m=\\sqrt2\\]<p>Keep the positive root inside the support. For a continuous variable, \\(P(X=a)=0\\); strict and inclusive endpoints have the same probability.</p></div>","sources":["session4.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["density","integration","cdf-continuous","moments","median-continuous"],"page":1,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: power rule, dx, constant factors, explicit powers and both bounds"]},"piecewise":{"title":"A density with two pieces","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> A triangular density is \\(f(x)=x\\) on (0,1) and \\(f(x)=2-x\\) on [1,2). Find its CDF and \\(P(.5<X<1.5)\\).</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Carry the completed first area into the second piece</h4>\\[F(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x}f(t)\\,dt\\]\\[0<x<1:\\quad F(x)=\\int_0^x t\\,dt=\\frac{x^2}{2}\\]\\[1\\le x<2:\\quad F(x)=\\frac12+\\int_1^x(2-t)\\,dt\\]\\[=\\frac12+\\left[2t-\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_1^x\\]\\[=\\frac12+\\left(2x-\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right)-\\left(2-\\frac12\\right)\\]\\[=2x-\\frac{x^2}{2}-1\\]\\[F(x)=0\\ (x\\le0),\\qquad F(x)=1\\ (x\\ge2)\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Subtract accumulated areas</h4>\\[P(a<X<b)=F(b)-F(a)\\]\\[F(.5)=\\frac{.5^2}{2}=.125\\]\\[F(1.5)=2(1.5)-\\frac{1.5^2}{2}-1=.875\\]\\[P(.5<X<1.5)=.875-.125=.75\\]<p>The CDF remains continuous at 1; only its formula changes. Do not restart accumulated probability at zero.</p></div>","sources":["session4.pdf"],"topics":["piecewise-density","piecewise-cdf"],"page":1,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: carry earlier area and subtract the lower bound explicitly"]},"endpoint":{"title":"Homework density: solve the unknown endpoint","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> \\(f(x)=.1+x\\) for \\(0<x\\le c\\), zero elsewhere. Find \\(c\\), the CDF, mean, variance and requested probabilities.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Force the full support area to equal one</h4>\\[\\int_0^c f(x)\\,dx=1\\]\\[\\int_0^c(.1+x)\\,dx=\\left[.1x+\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^c=1\\]\\[.1c+\\frac{c^2}{2}=1\\]\\[2(.1c)+2\\left(\\frac{c^2}{2}\\right)=2(1)\\]\\[.2c+c^2=2\\Rightarrow c^2+.2c-2=0\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Solve the quadratic; keep the valid endpoint</h4>\\[Ac^2+Bc+D=0:\\quad c=\\frac{-B\\pm\\sqrt{B^2-4AD}}{2A}\\]\\[c=\\frac{-.2\\pm\\sqrt{.2^2-4(1)(-2)}}{2}\\]\\[=\\frac{-.2\\pm\\sqrt{8.04}}{2}\\]\\[\\sqrt{8.04}=2.83549,\\quad c=\\frac{-.2+2.83549}{2}=1.31774\\]<p>Here \\(A=1,B=.2,D=-2\\). Keep the positive endpoint; the negative root \\(c_-=-1.51774\\) is outside this support.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Integrate only to the requested value</h4>\\[F(x)=\\int_0^x f(t)\\,dt\\]\\[F(x)=\\left[.1t+\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_0^x=.1x+\\frac{x^2}{2}\\]\\[F(x)=0\\ (x\\le0),\\qquad F(x)=1\\ (x\\ge c)\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Multiply by x and expand before integrating</h4>\\[E[X]=\\int_0^c x f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[x(.1+x)=.1x+x^2\\]\\[E[X]=\\int_0^c(.1x+x^2)\\,dx\\]\\[=\\left[.05x^2+\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^c\\]\\[=.05(1.31774)^2+\\frac{(1.31774)^3}{3}\\]\\[= .849556\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. Square x, not the entire density</h4>\\[E[X^2]=\\int_0^c x^2f(x)\\,dx\\]\\[x^2(.1+x)=.1x^2+x^3\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{30}+\\frac{x^4}{4}\\right]_0^c\\]\\[=\\frac{(1.31774)^3}{30}+\\frac{(1.31774)^4}{4}\\]\\[=.830089\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>6. Subtract squared mean from second moment</h4>\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-(E[X])^2\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=.830089-(.849556)^2\\]\\[=.108344\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>7. Use the CDF and complement</h4>\\[P(X>a)=1-F(a),\\quad P(X<b)=F(b)\\]\\[F(.5)=.1(.5)+\\frac{.5^2}{2}=.05+.125=.175\\]\\[P(X>.5)=1-F(.5)=1-.175=.825\\]\\[P(X<.6)=F(.6)=.1(.6)+\\frac{.6^2}{2}=.06+.18=.24\\]\\[P(X=.5)=0\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>8. Transform the mean without a new density</h4>\\[E[aX+b]=aE[X]+b\\]\\[E[2X+1]=2(.849556)+1=2.69911\\]</div>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session4.pdf"],"topics":["unknown-support","quadratic","continuous-expectation","continuous-variance"],"page":2,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: complete quadratic rearrangement, coefficient labels, root choice, boundary arithmetic"]},"uniform":{"title":"Uniform range and unknown coefficient","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> For the standard uniform distribution on (0,1), find its height, the probability below .5, its mean and variance.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Spread total area one across the interval</h4>\\[f(x)=\\frac1{b-a},\\quad P(u<X<v)=\\frac{v-u}{b-a}\\]\\[f(x)=\\frac1{1-0}=1,\\qquad P(0<X<.5)=\\frac{.5-0}{1-0}=.5\\]\\[E[X]=\\int_0^1x\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^1=\\frac12\\]\\[E[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}=\\frac{0+1}{2}=.5\\]\\[E[X^2]=\\int_0^1x^2\\,dx=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1=\\frac13\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-\\mu^2=\\frac13-\\frac14=\\frac{4-3}{12}\\]\\[=\\frac1{12}=\\frac{(b-a)^2}{12}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Unknown coefficient: factor it out of the area</h4>\\[f(x)=kx\\ (0<x<2),\\quad k\\int_0^2x\\,dx=1\\]\\[k\\left[\\frac{x^2}{2}\\right]_0^2=1\\Rightarrow 2k=1\\Rightarrow k=\\frac12\\]</div>","sources":["session4.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["uniform","unknown-coefficient"],"page":2,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: integrate both moments and subtract squared mean"]},"normal":{"title":"Glucose: left, between and right Normal areas","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Glucose \\(X\\sim N(95,8.7^2)\\). Find the probabilities below 85, from 85 to 115, and above 115. Here 8.7 is SD, not variance.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Write the Normal density and accumulated area</h4>\\[f_X(x)=\\frac1{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right]\\]\\[f_X(x)=\\frac1{8.7\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{(x-95)^2}{2(8.7)^2}\\right]\\]\\[F_X(x)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{x} f_X(t)\\,dt\\]<p>The CDF integral need not be solved algebraically; use the Normal table after standardizing.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Convert measurement cutoffs to SD units</h4>\\[z=\\frac{x-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\]\\[z_{85}=\\frac{85-95}{8.7}=-1.14943\\approx-1.15\\]\\[z_{115}=\\frac{115-95}{8.7}=2.29885\\approx2.30\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Read accumulated LEFT area in the Normal table</h4>\\[\\Phi(z)=P(Z\\le z),\\quad \\Phi(-z)=1-\\Phi(z)\\]\\[\\Phi(1.15)=.8749\\Rightarrow\\Phi(-1.15)=.1251\\]\\[\\Phi(2.30)=.9893\\]<p>The supplied table repeats complete z / LEFT-area pairs. Find 1.15 and read .8749 immediately beside it; find 2.30 and read .9893. For a negative z, subtract the positive-z area from one.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Use the CDF for each requested region</h4>\\[P(X<a)=\\Phi(z_a)\\]\\[P(X<85)=\\Phi(-1.15)=.1251\\]\\[P(a<X<b)=\\Phi(z_b)-\\Phi(z_a)\\]\\[P(85<X<115)=.9893-.1251=.8642\\]\\[P(X>b)=1-\\Phi(z_b)\\]\\[P(X>115)=1-.9893=.0107\\]</div><p><b>TI-84:</b> DISTR → normalcdf(lower,upper,95,8.7). Use −1E99/1E99 for infinite bounds. Calculator values are .12519, .864053 and .0107567; small table-rounding differences are expected.</p>\\[z_0=\\frac{0-95}{8.7}=-10.9195\\]\\[P(X<0)=\\Phi(-10.9195)\\approx0,\\quad P(X=95)=0\\]<p>A single exact value has zero area. The three disjoint regions below 85, from 85 to 115, and above 115 sum to one.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session5.pdf","tables/z_table_NormalDist.pdf"],"topics":["normal","normal-tails","normal-table"],"page":2,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: correct lookup for the actual supplied paired-column z table"]},"quantile":{"title":"Given a Normal area: recover the measurement","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Find the glucose 25th and 75th percentiles and central 95% interval.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Match cumulative area to a standard cutoff</h4>\\[\\Phi(z_p)=p,\\qquad x_p=\\mu+z_p\\sigma\\]\\[z_{.25}=-.67449,\\quad z_{.75}=+.67449\\]\\[x_{25}=95+(-.67449)(8.7)=89.1319\\]\\[x_{75}=95+(.67449)(8.7)=100.868\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Put half the omitted area in each tail</h4>\\[P(-z_*<Z<z_*)=.95\\Rightarrow\\Phi(z_*)=.975\\]\\[z_*=1.96\\]\\[x_L=\\mu-z_*\\sigma=95-1.96(8.7)=77.948\\]\\[x_U=x_{97.5}=\\mu+z_*\\sigma=95+1.96(8.7)=112.052\\]<p><b>TI-84:</b> invNorm(left area,95,8.7). A right-tail area .025 means LEFT area .975. Use the specified tail; do not enter a percentage such as 97.5.</p></div><img class=\"v7-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-normal.svg\" alt=\"Normal density and cumulative probability with shaded intervals\"><p><b>Sketch:</b> density is a bell centered at \\(\\mu\\); mark cutoffs and shade area. Normal CDF rises smoothly from 0 to 1, crosses .5 at \\(\\mu\\), and has no jumps. Larger SD widens the density.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session5.pdf"],"topics":["inverse-normal","normal-graph"],"page":2,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"continuous-graph":{"title":"Draw a continuous PDF and CDF","html":"<img class=\"v7-plot\" src=\"figures/v4-continuous.svg\" alt=\"Homework density .1 plus x and its continuous cumulative function\"><p><b>Density \\(f(x)=.1+x\\):</b> zero outside (0,\\(c\\)]; inside, draw a straight rising line from height .1 to .1+\\(c\\). Shade an interval under the line. \\(f(.5)=.6\\) is a height, not a probability.</p><p><b>CDF:</b> zero before 0, curve \\(F(x)=.1x+x^2/2\\) inside, then flat at 1 after \\(c\\). It is continuous and increasing. Its slope at each point is the density height. Changing formulas can create corners; a continuous density has no point-mass jumps.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session4.pdf"],"topics":["continuous-graph"],"page":2,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"sampling":{"title":"Average of n: sampling distribution and CLT","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Individual responses have mean 4 and SD 1.5. For independent samples of 50, find the probability that the sample mean is between 3.5 and 3.8.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Keep the center; reduce variance for averaging</h4>\\[E[\\bar X]=\\mu,\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\]\\[E[\\bar X]=4,\\quad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar X)=\\frac{1.5^2}{50}=.045\\]\\[SD(\\bar X)=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}=\\frac{1.5}{\\sqrt{50}}=.212132\\]<p>\\(\\bar X\\) (random sample mean); \\(\\bar x\\) (one observed mean). If individuals are Normal, means are Normal at any sample size. Otherwise the CLT gives an approximately Normal mean for sufficiently large independent samples (course rule: \\(n\\ge30\\)).</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Divide each mean cutoff by the MEAN spread</h4>\\[z=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n}\\]\\[z_L=\\frac{3.5-4}{.212132}=-2.35702\\]\\[z_U=\\frac{3.8-4}{.212132}=-.942809\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Subtract the accumulated areas</h4>\\[P(a<\\bar X<b)=\\Phi(z_b)-\\Phi(z_a)\\]\\[\\Phi(-.942809)=.172889\\]\\[\\Phi(-2.35702)=.00921106\\]\\[P(3.5<\\bar X<3.8)=.172889-.00921106\\]\\[=.163678\\]<p>Rounded table cutoffs −.94 and −2.36 give .1736−.0091=.1645. For an individual observation, use \\(\\sigma\\) itself; do not divide it by \\(\\sqrt n\\).</p></div>","sources":["session10.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["sampling","clt","sem-probability"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"linear":{"title":"Shift, scale, and independent sums","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> For the homework glucose variable, find \\(E[X-26]\\). A constant changes the center, while multiplying a variable also changes its spread.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Apply coefficients directly to the mean</h4>\\[E[aX+b]=a\\mu+b,\\quad\\operatorname{Var}(aX+b)=a^2\\sigma^2\\]\\[E[X-26]=95-26=69\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X-26)=1^2(8.7^2)=75.69\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. For independent variables, ADD variances</h4>\\[E[aX+bY]=a\\mu_X+b\\mu_Y\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY)=a^2\\sigma_X^2+b^2\\sigma_Y^2\\]<p>For two independent measurements with the glucose distribution:</p></div>\\[E[X+Y]=95+95=190\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X+Y)=8.7^2+8.7^2=151.38\\]\\[\\operatorname{Var}(X-Y)=8.7^2+(-1)^2(8.7^2)=151.38\\]<p>Subtraction still adds variance because its coefficient is squared. The independent-sum variance rule does not apply to \\(X+X\\): that is \\(2X\\), whose variance is \\(4\\sigma^2\\).</p>\\[E[h(X)]=\\sum_xh(x)p(x)\\quad\\text{(discrete)}\\]\\[E[h(X)]=\\int h(x)f(x)\\,dx\\quad\\text{(continuous)}\\]","sources":["session3.pdf","session10.pdf"],"topics":["linear-transform","independent-sum"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: general expectation of a function"]},"precision":{"title":"Sample size and precision of an average","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> The height sample has SD 3.02765 and SEM .957427 with 10 observations. How does the estimated SEM change with four times as many observations?</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Compare sample sizes with individual spread held fixed</h4>\\[SEM_{\\rm new}=SEM_{\\rm old}\\sqrt{\\frac{n_{\\rm old}}{n_{\\rm new}}}\\]\\[n_{\\rm new}=4(10)=40\\]\\[SEM_{\\rm new}=.957427\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{40}}=.478714\\]<p>Quadrupling sample size halves SEM. For a target SEM, rearrange \\(SEM=s/\\sqrt n\\): \\(n=(s/SEM_{\\rm target})^2\\), then round upward. Larger samples improve the mean’s precision; they do not necessarily reduce individual variation.</p></div>","sources":["session10.pdf"],"topics":["precision","sample-size"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: estimator properties and uncertainty in sample variance"]},"one-t":{"title":"One mean: student LDL against a fixed reference","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Sixteen USC undergraduates have mean LDL 103 mg/dL and SD 16 mg/dL. The faculty/staff reference mean is 115 mg/dL. Assuming Normal independent responses, is the undergraduate population mean different at \\(\\alpha=.05\\)?</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the population hypotheses</h4>\\[H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0,\\qquad H_A:\\mu\\ne\\mu_0\\]\\[H_0:\\mu=115,\\qquad H_A:\\mu\\ne115\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the undergraduate population mean equals 115 under the null; it differs from 115 under the alternative. \\(\\mu_0\\) is the fixed reference, not another sample mean.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Estimate the mean’s standard error</h4>\\[SE=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt n}\\]\\[SE=\\frac{16}{\\sqrt{16}}=\\frac{16}{4}=4\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Standardize the observed departure</h4>\\[t=\\frac{\\bar x-\\mu_0}{s/\\sqrt n}\\]\\[t=\\frac{103-115}{4}=\\frac{-12}{4}=-3\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Use the one-sample degrees of freedom</h4>\\[\\nu=n-1,\\qquad |t|>t_{\\rm crit}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]\\[\\nu=16-1=15\\]\\[t_{\\rm crit}=t_{.025,15}=2.13145\\]\\[|t|=3>2.13145\\]<p><b>Table:</b> row 15, total two-tail column .05 (printed cutoff 2.131). Reject the null: undergraduate population mean LDL differs from 115 at .05; the sample direction is lower.</p></div><p><b>p-value:</b> \\(p=2P(T_{15}\\ge3)=.00897274\\). Compare \\(p\\) directly with .05. It is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true.</p>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session8.pdf"],"topics":["one-sample-t","hypothesis-words"],"page":3,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"pooled-t":{"title":"Two independent means: cognitive response","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Control: \\(n_1=22\\), mean 49, SD 8.4. Intervention: \\(n_2=27\\), mean 57, SD 9.5. Test whether the population means differ at .05. Assume independent Normal populations with a common variance.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the hypotheses for the two populations</h4>\\[H_0:\\mu_1=\\mu_2,\\qquad H_A:\\mu_1\\ne\\mu_2\\]<p><b>In words:</b> control and intervention have equal population means under the null; their population means differ under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Pool VARIANCES, weighted by their df</h4>\\[s_p^2=\\frac{(n_1-1)s_1^2+(n_2-1)s_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}\\]\\[s_1^2=8.4^2=70.56,\\qquad s_2^2=9.5^2=90.25\\]\\[s_p^2=\\frac{21(70.56)+26(90.25)}{22+27-2}\\]\\[=\\frac{1481.76+2346.50}{47}=81.4523\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Compute the standard error of the difference</h4>\\[SE_{\\rm diff}=\\sqrt{s_p^2\\left(\\frac1{n_1}+\\frac1{n_2}\\right)}\\]\\[SE_{\\rm diff}=\\sqrt{81.4523\\left(\\frac1{22}+\\frac1{27}\\right)}\\]\\[=2.59213\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Divide the observed mean gap by its SE</h4>\\[t=\\frac{\\bar x_1-\\bar x_2}{SE_{\\rm diff}}\\]\\[t=\\frac{49-57}{2.59213}=-3.08627\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. Compare with a two-sided cutoff</h4>\\[\\nu=n_1+n_2-2,\\quad |t|>t_{\\rm crit}\\]\\[\\nu=22+27-2=47\\]\\[|t|=3.08627>t_{.025,47}=2.01174\\]<p>If row 47 is missing, neighboring rows 46 and 48 give 2.013 and 2.011; the decision is unchanged. Reject equal population means. The intervention has the larger sample mean. \\(p=.00339345\\). Unequal sample sizes do not prevent pooling when common variance is assumed.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session8.pdf"],"topics":["pooled-t","unequal-sample-sizes"],"page":3,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"t-output":{"title":"Read supplied t-test output","html":"<pre>Welch Two Sample t-test\nt = -4.5298, df = 15.611\np-value = 0.0003625\nmean of x   mean of y\n64.5        69.7</pre><p>Read \\(t\\) (observed statistic), df (reference distribution) and p-value. \\(.0003625<.05\\): reject equal population means. The negative statistic matches the first mean being smaller. Welch output can have fractional df; use the df printed in that output.</p><pre>Two Sample t-test\nt = -4.5298, df = 18\np-value = 0.0002594</pre><p>“Two Sample t-test” with pooled variances uses \\(\\nu=n_1+n_2-2\\). The course’s hand calculations use the stated common-variance model; interpret supplied output according to its printed test name.</p>","sources":["session8.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["t-output"],"page":3,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"anova":{"title":"Three filgrastim doses: balanced ANOVA","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Fifteen patients per filgrastim dose have neutrophil concentrations measured in \\(10^9\\) cells/L. Test whether the three population means are equal at .05, then compare all dose pairs as one family.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Dose</th><th>Mean</th><th>SD</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low</td><td>3.7</td><td>1.1</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>4.6</td><td>1.4</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>5.2</td><td>2.1</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the overall population hypotheses</h4>\\[H_0:\\mu_1=\\mu_2=\\mu_3\\]\\[H_A:\\text{not all population means are equal}\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the three dose population means are equal under the null; at least one differs under the alternative. Assume independent Normal responses and equal population variances.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Average the group variances for within spread</h4>\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=\\frac{\\sum_{j=1}^m s_j^2}{m}\\]\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=\\frac{1.1^2+1.4^2+2.1^2}{3}\\]\\[=\\frac{1.21+1.96+4.41}{3}=2.52667\\]<p>\\(m=3\\) (number of groups); \\(n=15\\) (people per group); \\(s_j\\) (SD in group \\(j\\)).</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Average the means; square their deviations</h4>\\[\\bar{\\bar x}=\\frac{\\sum_j\\bar x_j}{m}\\]\\[\\bar{\\bar x}=\\frac{3.7+4.6+5.2}{3}=4.5\\]\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_j(\\bar x_j-\\bar{\\bar x})^2&=(3.7-4.5)^2\\\\&\\quad+(4.6-4.5)^2+(5.2-4.5)^2\\\\&=.64+.01+.49=1.14\\end{aligned}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Divide by m−1, then multiply by group size</h4>\\[s_{\\bar x}^2=\\frac{\\sum_j(\\bar x_j-\\bar{\\bar x})^2}{m-1},\\quad s_{\\rm bet}^2=ns_{\\bar x}^2\\]\\[s_{\\bar x}^2=\\frac{1.14}{3-1}=.57\\]\\[s_{\\rm bet}^2=15(.57)=8.55\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. Divide between by within</h4>\\[F=\\frac{s_{\\rm bet}^2}{s_{\\rm wit}^2}\\]\\[F=\\frac{8.55}{2.52667}=3.38391\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>6. Use numerator and denominator df in that order</h4>\\[\\nu_1=m-1,\\quad\\nu_2=m(n-1)\\]\\[\\nu_1=3-1=2,\\quad\\nu_2=3(15-1)=42\\]\\[F=3.38391>F_{.05;2,42}=3.21994\\]<p><b>Table:</b> right-tail .05; numerator df 2 across the top, denominator df 42 down the side, lightface value 3.22. Reject all-equal population means; at least one dose population differs. \\(p=.0433947\\). The pairwise calculations are separate from the overall decision.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session6.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["balanced-anova","anova-variance","anova-df"],"page":3,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"anova-output":{"title":"Use supplied ANOVA output for follow-up tests","html":"<pre class=\"aov-output\">            Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value Pr(&gt;F)  \nGroup        2  779.4   389.7   5.832 0.0106 *\nResiduals   19 1269.5    66.8                 </pre><p><b>Read:</b> Group df 2 = F numerator df; Residuals df 19 = denominator and pairwise df. Residuals Mean Sq 66.8 = within variance; F value 5.832 = test statistic; Pr(&gt;F) .0106 = p-value.</p>\\[F=\\frac{MS_{\\rm Group}}{MS_{\\rm Residual}}=5.832\\quad\\text{(unrounded inputs)}\\]<p>At .05 reject all-equal population means; at .01 do not reject. For requested pairs retain residual variance and df, with the actual pair sample sizes.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>If residual STANDARD ERROR is supplied, square it</h4>\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=(\\text{residual standard error})^2\\]\\[s_{\\rm wit}^2=(8.17407)^2=66.8154\\approx66.8\\]<p>Residual standard error is an SD; residual Mean Sq is already a variance.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session7.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["anova-output","assumptions"],"page":3,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: full summary(aov) fields, alpha comparison and residual-SD conversion"]},"bonferroni":{"title":"Filgrastim pairs: Bonferroni, every comparison","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Using the filgrastim ANOVA, compare all three dose pairs at family \\(\\alpha=.05\\).</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the hypothesis for EACH dose pair</h4>\\[H_{0,ij}:\\mu_i=\\mu_j,\\quad H_{A,ij}:\\mu_i\\ne\\mu_j\\]<p><b>In words:</b> each null says the two named dose populations have equal means; each alternative says those population means differ.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Divide family alpha by the requested pair count</h4>\\[k=\\begin{cases}m(m-1)/2&\\text{all pairs}\\\\m-1&\\text{vs control}\\end{cases}\\]\\[\\alpha_{\\rm pair}=\\frac{\\alpha_{\\rm family}}{k}\\]\\[k=\\frac{3(3-1)}2=3,\\quad\\alpha_{\\rm pair}=\\frac{.05}{3}=.0166667\\]\\[\\nu=m(n-1)=3(14)=42\\]\\[t_{\\rm crit}=t_{\\alpha_{\\rm pair}/2,42}=2.49367\\]<p>Use total two-tail probability .0166667, or one-tail .00833333, once. \\(k\\) counts the specified comparison family, not automatically every possible pair.</p></div><p><b>TI-84, missing table column:</b> DISTR → invT. Enter the LEFT cumulative area and original residual df:</p>\\[t_{\\rm crit}=\\operatorname{invT}(1-\\alpha_{\\rm pair}/2,\\nu)\\]\\[=\\operatorname{invT}(1-(.05/3)/2,42)=2.49367\\]<div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Compute the shared SE using the original within variance</h4>\\[SE_{ij}=\\sqrt{s_{\\rm wit}^2\\left(\\frac1{n_i}+\\frac1{n_j}\\right)}\\]\\[SE_{ij}=\\sqrt{2.52667\\left(\\frac1{15}+\\frac1{15}\\right)}\\]\\[=\\sqrt{\\frac{2(2.52667)}{15}}=.580421\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Low versus Medium</h4>\\[|t_{ij}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_i-\\bar x_j|}{SE_{ij}}\\]\\[|t_{LM}|=\\frac{|3.7-4.6|}{.580421}=1.5506\\]\\[1.5506<2.49367\\Rightarrow\\text{do not reject }H_{0,LM}\\]</div><p><b>Remaining dose pairs continue at the top of the next column.</b></p>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session9.pdf"],"topics":["bonferroni","all-pairs","pairwise-equations"],"page":3,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: requested comparison family, all pairs versus control"]},"holm":{"title":"Holm chi-square: the three control comparisons","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> The homework mortality table has three treatments and one control. The three Yates-corrected treatment–control statistics are 18.2341, 9.49443 and 4.68951. Use Holm at family .05.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Order the evidence, then reduce the remaining family</h4>\\[\\alpha_j=\\frac{\\alpha}{k-j+1}\\]\\[k=3:\\quad\\alpha_1=.05/3=.0166667\\]\\[\\alpha_2=.05/2=.025,\\qquad\\alpha_3=.05/1=.05\\]<p>Order p-values from smallest to largest. For the same df, order \\(\\chi^2\\) statistics largest to smallest. <b>In words:</b> each null says that treatment and control have equal mortality probabilities; each alternative says they differ.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Compare each ordered statistic with its own cutoff</h4>\\[\\chi_j^2>\\chi^2_{\\alpha_j,1}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_{0,j}\\]\\[18.2341>6.635>\\chi^2_{.0166667,1}\\quad\\text{reject}\\]<p>The .0166667 column is absent: exceeding the stricter .01 cutoff 6.635 already establishes this first rejection.</p>\\[9.49443>\\chi^2_{.025,1}=5.02389\\]\\[4.68951>\\chi^2_{.05,1}=3.84146\\]<p>All three treatment–control differences are supported. Stop at the first comparison that fails; retain it and every remaining null.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session9.pdf"],"topics":["holm","chi-square-posthoc"],"page":3,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: handle the absent chi-square alpha column with a printed-table bound"]},"tables":{"title":"Read the supplied tables and test tails","html":"<p><b>z:</b> complete z and adjacent cumulative LEFT area. Negative z uses symmetry. <b>t:</b> df row, total TWO-TAILED alpha column; .05 means .05 here. <b>F:</b> numerator df across top, denominator down the side; upper-tail alpha. <b>Chi-square:</b> df row, right-tail alpha; the two supplied chi-square tables describe the same distribution.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Bound a p-value between printed table columns</h4>\\[t_{\\rm two\\ tail\\ .10,4}<|t|<t_{\\rm two\\ tail\\ .05,4}\\]\\[2.132<2.58630<2.776\\Rightarrow .05<p_{\\rm two\\ sided}<.10\\]<p>Missing df? Compare both neighboring-row cutoffs. Above both → reject; below both → do not reject; between them → obtain an exact cutoff or supplied p-value.</p></div><p><b>TI-84:</b> positive t cutoff \\(\\operatorname{invT}(1-\\alpha/2,df)\\); two-sided p-value \\(2\\operatorname{tcdf}(|t|,1E99,df)\\). Halve alpha for the invT area conversion only; do not halve the supplied two-tail table heading.</p>","sources":["session15.pdf","session9.pdf","tables/t_table.pdf","tables/F_table.pdf","tables/Chi_Squared_Table1.pdf","tables/Chi_Squared_Table2.pdf"],"topics":["table-reading","tails"],"page":3,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: printed table tails, p-value bracketing, missing rows and TI-84 conversions"]},"z-yates":{"title":"Two proportions: thrombi and Yates z","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Thrombi occur in 18 of 25 placebo patients and 6 of 19 aspirin patients. Is the population thrombus probability different between groups at .05?</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the population hypotheses and sample fractions</h4>\\[H_0:\\pi_1=\\pi_2,\\quad H_A:\\pi_1\\ne\\pi_2\\]\\[\\hat p_1=\\frac{x_1}{n_1}=\\frac{18}{25}=.72\\]\\[\\hat p_2=\\frac{x_2}{n_2}=\\frac6{19}=.315789\\]<p><b>In words:</b> placebo and aspirin have equal thrombus probabilities under the null; the probabilities differ under the alternative. \\(x_i\\) is the event count; \\(n_i\\) is that group’s total.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Pool event counts for the null probability</h4>\\[\\hat p=\\frac{x_1+x_2}{n_1+n_2}\\]\\[\\hat p=\\frac{18+6}{25+19}=\\frac{24}{44}=.545455\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Calculate the null SE and half-count adjustment</h4>\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)\\left(\\frac1{n_1}+\\frac1{n_2}\\right)}\\]\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{.545455(.454545)\\left(\\frac1{25}+\\frac1{19}\\right)}\\]\\[=.151547\\]\\[c_Y=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1{n_1}+\\frac1{n_2}\\right)\\]\\[c_Y=\\frac12\\left(\\frac1{25}+\\frac1{19}\\right)=.0463158\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Shrink the absolute observed gap, then standardize</h4>\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{\\max(0,|\\hat p_1-\\hat p_2|-c_Y)}{SE_0}\\]\\[D_Y=\\max(0,|.72-.315789|-.0463158)\\]\\[=.357895\\]\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{.357895}{.151547}=2.36161\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. Make the two-sided decision</h4>\\[|z_Y|>z_{\\alpha/2}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_0\\]\\[2.36161>1.95996\\]<p><b>Table:</b> Normal cumulative .975, or t-table infinite-df row total two-tail .05. Reject equal population thrombus probabilities; aspirin has the lower observed proportion.</p></div>","sources":["session11.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["two-proportions","yates-z"],"page":4,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: authentic supplied chi-square output and corrected z/chi-square equivalence"]},"chi2":{"title":"Folate: every expected count and corrected cell","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Is malformation occurrence associated with folate use? Test at .05. Apply Yates to this 2×2 table.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Event</th><th>No event</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Folate</td><td>4</td><td>471</td><td>475</td></tr><tr><td>No folate</td><td>27</td><td>498</td><td>525</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>31</td><td>969</td><td>1000</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State independence in the population</h4>\\[H_0:\\text{group and outcome are independent}\\]\\[H_A:\\text{group and outcome are associated}\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the malformation probability is the same with and without folate under the null; it differs under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Build ALL expected counts from margins</h4>\\[E_{ij}=\\frac{R_iC_j}{N}\\]\\[E_{11}=\\frac{475(31)}{1000}=14.725\\]\\[E_{12}=\\frac{475(969)}{1000}=460.275\\]\\[E_{21}=\\frac{525(31)}{1000}=16.275\\]\\[E_{22}=\\frac{525(969)}{1000}=508.725\\]<p>\\(R_i\\) (row total); \\(C_j\\) (column total); \\(N\\) (grand total); \\(O_{ij}\\) (observed count).</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Calculate EVERY corrected cell contribution</h4>\\[c_{ij}=\\frac{\\max(0,|O_{ij}-E_{ij}|-.5)^2}{E_{ij}}\\]\\[c_{11}=\\frac{(|4-14.725|-.5)^2}{14.725}=7.10021\\]\\[c_{12}=\\frac{(|471-460.275|-.5)^2}{460.275}=.227148\\]\\[c_{21}=\\frac{(|27-16.275|-.5)^2}{16.275}=6.424\\]\\[c_{22}=\\frac{(|498-508.725|-.5)^2}{508.725}=.205515\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Add contributions; compare on the right tail</h4>\\[\\chi_Y^2=\\sum_i\\sum_j c_{ij},\\quad\\nu=(r-1)(c-1)\\]\\[\\chi_Y^2=7.10021+.227148\\]\\[\\qquad+6.424+.205515=13.9569\\]\\[\\nu=(2-1)(2-1)=1\\]\\[13.9569>\\chi^2_{.05,1}=3.84146\\]<p>Reject independence: folate use and malformation occurrence are associated. \\(p=.00018705\\). Expected counts use the null margins; the observed counts remain unchanged. Yates shrinks the absolute gap before squaring.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session12.pdf"],"topics":["chi-square-2x2","all-expected-cells","all-contributions"],"page":4,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"large-chi":{"title":"Three groups: uncorrected larger-table chi-square","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Compare depression occurrence across three groups. Observed event/no-event counts are (36,131), (41,132), (75,104). Test at .05.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the hypotheses; total rows and columns</h4>\\[H_0:\\pi_1=\\pi_2=\\pi_3,\\quad H_A:\\text{not all }\\pi_i\\text{ equal}\\]\\[(R_1,R_2,R_3)=(167,173,179)\\]\\[(C_1,C_2)=(152,367),\\qquad N=519\\]<p><b>In words:</b> all three population event probabilities are equal under the null; at least one differs under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Calculate ALL six null expected counts</h4>\\[E_{ij}=\\frac{R_iC_j}{N}\\]\\[E_{11}=\\frac{167(152)}{519}=48.9094\\]\\[E_{12}=\\frac{167(367)}{519}=118.091\\]\\[E_{21}=\\frac{173(152)}{519}=50.6667\\]\\[E_{22}=\\frac{173(367)}{519}=122.333\\]\\[E_{31}=\\frac{179(152)}{519}=52.4239\\]\\[E_{32}=\\frac{179(367)}{519}=126.576\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Calculate ALL six contributions (no Yates)</h4>\\[c_{ij}=\\frac{(O_{ij}-E_{ij})^2}{E_{ij}}\\]\\[c_{11}=\\frac{(36-48.9094)^2}{48.9094}=3.40739\\]\\[c_{12}=\\frac{(131-118.091)^2}{118.091}=1.41124\\]\\[c_{21}=\\frac{(41-50.6667)^2}{50.6667}=1.8443\\]\\[c_{22}=\\frac{(132-122.333)^2}{122.333}=.763851\\]\\[c_{31}=\\frac{(75-52.4239)^2}{52.4239}=9.7223\\]\\[c_{32}=\\frac{(104-126.576)^2}{126.576}=4.02667\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. Add and select the larger-table df</h4>\\[\\chi^2=\\sum c_{ij},\\quad\\nu=(r-1)(c-1)\\]\\[\\chi^2=3.40739+1.41124+1.8443\\]\\[\\quad+.763851+9.7223+4.02667\\]\\[=21.1757\\]\\[\\nu=(3-1)(2-1)=2\\]\\[21.1757>\\chi^2_{.05,2}=5.99146\\]<p>Reject equal population event probabilities. The statistic alone does not identify a particular differing group. Yates is applied to 2×2 tables, not this 3×2 table.</p></div><pre>X-squared = 21.176, df = 2\np-value = 2.522e-05</pre>\\[\\texttt{2.522e-05}=.00002522\\]","sources":["session12.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["chi-square-larger","all-six-cells"],"page":4,"column":2,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: authentic larger-table output and scientific notation"]},"gof":{"title":"One list: goodness of fit to specified probabilities","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Counts are 255, 125 and 90. Do they fit target proportions .60, .25 and .15 at .05?</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. State the target distribution; find expected counts</h4>\\[H_0:(\\pi_1,\\pi_2,\\pi_3)=(.60,.25,.15)\\]\\[H_A:\\text{at least one population proportion differs}\\]\\[N=255+125+90=470,\\quad E_i=Np_i\\]\\[E_1=470(.60)=282\\]\\[E_2=470(.25)=117.5,\\quad E_3=470(.15)=70.5\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the population follows the specified target proportions under the null; it does not follow that distribution under the alternative.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Work EVERY category, then sum</h4>\\[c_i=\\frac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i},\\quad\\chi^2=\\sum_i c_i\\]\\[c_1=\\frac{(255-282)^2}{282}=2.58511\\]\\[c_2=\\frac{(125-117.5)^2}{117.5}=.478723\\]\\[c_3=\\frac{(90-70.5)^2}{70.5}=5.39362\\]\\[\\chi^2=2.58511+.478723+5.39362\\]\\[=8.45745\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Use category count minus one</h4>\\[\\nu=k-1,\\quad\\chi^2>\\chi^2_{\\alpha,\\nu}\\]\\[\\nu=3-1=2,\\qquad8.45744681>5.99146\\]<p>Reject the target proportions at .05 (p = .01457). Use specified target probabilities, not observed fractions. Targets sum to one; this one-list fit test has no Yates correction.</p></div>","sources":["session12.pdf"],"topics":["goodness-of-fit"],"page":4,"column":1,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: authentic goodness-of-fit output"]},"binary":{"title":"One binary proportion: SD versus SE","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Eight of 61 patients have the event. Estimate the event proportion and its SE.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Use event count divided by all patients</h4>\\[\\hat p=\\frac{x}{n},\\quad s_{\\rm binary}=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}\\]\\[\\hat p=\\frac8{61}=.131148\\]\\[s_{\\rm binary}=\\sqrt{.131148(.868852)}=.337562\\]\\[SE(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}n}\\]\\[SE(\\hat p)=\\sqrt{\\frac{.131148(.868852)}{61}}=.0432203\\]<p>The SE is uncertainty in the estimated proportion, not the spread of individual zero/one outcomes.</p></div>","sources":["session11.pdf"],"topics":["binary-proportion","proportion-se"],"page":2,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"risk":{"title":"Followed groups: risk, ARR and NNT","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> In the aspirin study, event counts are 6/19 treated and 18/25 controls. Quantify the treatment effect.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Find BOTH event risks from group totals</h4>\\[r_g=\\frac{\\text{events in group }g}{\\text{all people in group }g}\\]\\[r_T=\\frac6{19}=.315789\\]\\[r_C=\\frac{18}{25}=.72\\]<p>\\(T\\) (treatment); \\(C\\) (control). Risks compare events with all group members, including nonevents.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Divide for relative risk; subtract for reduction</h4>\\[RR=\\frac{r_T}{r_C},\\qquad ARR=r_C-r_T\\]\\[RR=\\frac{6/19}{18/25}=.438596\\]\\[ARR=\\frac{18}{25}-\\frac6{19}=.404211\\]<p>Treatment risk is about .439 times control risk. The absolute reduction is .4042, or 40.42 percentage points; it is not the relative reduction.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Take the reciprocal of ABSOLUTE risk reduction</h4>\\[NNT=\\frac1{ARR}\\]\\[NNT=\\frac1{.404211}=2.47396\\Rightarrow3\\]<p>Round upward to a whole person: treat 3 people to prevent one additional event over the studied interval. Use proportions, not percentage numbers.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>4. If treatment increases harm, use NNH</h4>\\[ARI=r_T-r_C,\\quad NNH=\\frac1{ARI}\\]\\[ARI=.08-.03=.05,\\qquad NNH=\\frac1{.05}=20\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Small baseline risks: relative benefit versus absolute benefit</h4>\\[RR=\\frac{r_T}{r_C},\\quad RRR=1-RR,\\quad ARR=r_C-r_T\\]\\[r_C=10/1000=.010,\\quad r_T=8/1000=.008\\]\\[RR=\\frac{.008}{.010}=.8,\\quad RRR=1-.8=.20\\]\\[ARR=.010-.008=.002,\\quad NNT=1/.002=500\\]\\[\\frac{NNT}{NNH}=\\frac{500}{20}=25\\]<p>A 20% relative reduction here is .2 percentage points absolute. With NNH 20, 25 additional harms accompany one additional prevented event in this comparison.</p></div>","sources":["session13.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["risk","arr","nnt","nnh"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording","V5: low-baseline-risk NNT/NNH tradeoff"]},"folate-risk":{"title":"Folate: name the numerator risk","html":"<div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Compare no-folate risk with folate risk</h4>\\[r_g=\\frac{x_g}{n_g},\\qquad RR_{A/B}=\\frac{r_A}{r_B}\\]\\[r_{\\rm no\\ folate}=\\frac{27}{525}=.0514286\\]\\[r_{\\rm folate}=\\frac4{475}=.00842105\\]\\[RR_{\\rm no/yes}=\\frac{27/525}{4/475}=6.10714\\]\\[RR_{\\rm yes/no}=\\frac{4/475}{27/525}=.163743\\]<p>No-folate risk is about 6.11 times folate risk; reversing the comparison gives the reciprocal. State which group is in the numerator.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session13.pdf"],"topics":["risk-orientation"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"odds":{"title":"Selected cases and controls: odds ratio","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Among cases, 50 were exposed and 14 unexposed. Among controls, 43 were exposed and 35 unexposed. Estimate the exposure odds ratio.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Exposed</th><th>Unexposed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cases</td><td>50</td><td>14</td></tr><tr><td>Controls</td><td>43</td><td>35</td></tr></tbody></table><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>Divide exposed by unexposed in EACH selected group</h4>\\[OR=\\frac{a/b}{c/d}=\\frac{ad}{bc}\\]\\[O_{\\rm case}=\\frac{50}{14}=3.57143\\]\\[O_{\\rm control}=\\frac{43}{35}=1.22857\\]\\[OR=\\frac{50/14}{43/35}=\\frac{50(35)}{14(43)}\\]\\[=\\frac{1750}{602}=2.90698\\]<p>Cases have about 2.91 times the exposure odds of controls. Case–control selection fixes how many cases are sampled; these sample fractions do not estimate disease risk. Use OR for this design, not RR.</p></div>","sources":["session13.pdf"],"topics":["odds-ratio","case-control"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"design":{"title":"Study design: what was assigned and followed?","html":"<p><b>Randomized trial:</b> assign treatment, then follow outcomes. <b>Cohort:</b> follow exposure groups without random assignment. Both support event risks/RR. <b>Case–control:</b> select by outcome and assess earlier exposure; use OR. <b>Parallel:</b> different participants receive treatments. <b>Crossover:</b> the same participants receive treatments in different periods; consider stability and carryover. <b>Masking:</b> hide assignment from participants, treatment/evaluation staff or statistical staff. A sham mimics the procedure experience without its active component.</p>","sources":["session10.pdf","session12.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["design","chi-square-output"],"page":4,"column":3,"provenance":["V6: explicit equations, operations and hypothesis wording"]},"r-normal":{"title":"Read supplied Normal-function output","html":"<p><b>pnorm:</b> cutoff → area; <b>qnorm:</b> area → cutoff. Default LEFT; lower.tail=FALSE means RIGHT.</p><pre>pnorm(85,95,8.7)\n[1] 0.12519\npnorm(115,95,8.7,lower.tail=FALSE)\n[1] 0.0107567\nqnorm(.975,95,8.7)\n[1] 112.052</pre><p>Right area .025 and left .975 give the same cutoff. The third argument is SD; use SEM for an average.</p>","sources":["hw1.pdf","session5.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["normal-output","lower-tail"],"provenance":["V5: authentic Normal-output examples; interpretation only"],"page":2,"column":3},"holm-t":{"title":"Holm t comparisons: stop at the first failure","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Five comparisons with G1 have \\(\\nu=46\\). Their ordered absolute t values are 3.3, 2.9, 2.3, 2.2 and 2.1. Test the family at .05.</p>\\[H_{0,1j}:\\mu_1=\\mu_j,\\quad H_{A,1j}:\\mu_1\\ne\\mu_j\\]<p><b>In words:</b> each null says G1 and that named group have equal population means; its alternative says their means differ.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Order strongest evidence first; adjust the remaining family</h4>\\[\\alpha_j=\\frac{\\alpha_{\\rm family}}{k-j+1}\\]\\[\\alpha_1=\\frac{.05}{5}=.0100,\\quad\\alpha_2=\\frac{.05}{4}=.0125\\]\\[\\alpha_3=\\frac{.05}{3}=.0166667\\]<p>Sort p-values smallest first; with equal df, sort absolute t largest first.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Check each ordered pair using its own two-sided cutoff</h4>\\[|t_j|>t_{\\alpha_j/2,\\nu}\\Rightarrow\\text{reject and continue}\\]\\[G1:G6:\\quad3.3>t_{.005,46}=2.687\\quad\\text{reject}\\]\\[G1:G3:\\quad2.9>t_{.00625,46}=2.59973\\quad\\text{reject}\\]\\[G1:G5:\\quad2.3<t_{.00833333,46}=2.48469\\quad\\text{STOP}\\]<p>Retain G1:G5 and every remaining null (G1:G4, G1:G2), without further tests. Only G1:G6 and G1:G3 are supported as different at family .05. Use invT(1−alpha/2,46) when the table column is missing.</p></div>","sources":["session9.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["holm-t","holm-stop"],"provenance":["V5: full ordered t example including the stopping decision"],"page":3,"column":3},"dioxin":{"title":"Rounded proportions: the dioxin homework variant","html":"<p><b>Question.</b> Compare female-birth probabilities after father-only (.565 of 186) versus mother-only (.455 of 220) dioxin exposure. The combined sample has 205 female births among 406 babies; use .05.</p>\\[H_0:\\pi_F=\\pi_M,\\quad H_A:\\pi_F\\ne\\pi_M\\]<p><b>In words:</b> the two populations have equal female-birth probabilities under the null; they differ under the alternative.</p><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>1. Pool the exact combined count; keep the stated group fractions</h4>\\[\\hat p=\\frac{\\text{total events}}{n_1+n_2}\\]\\[\\hat p=\\frac{205}{186+220}=\\frac{205}{406}=.504926\\]<p>Do not reconstruct integer group counts from rounded proportions.</p></div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>2. Find the null SE and half-count correction</h4>\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)(1/n_1+1/n_2)}\\]\\[SE_0=\\sqrt{.504926(.495074)(1/186+1/220)}\\]\\[=.0498017\\]\\[c_Y=\\tfrac12(1/n_1+1/n_2)\\]\\[c_Y=.5(1/186+1/220)=.0049609\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>3. Shrink the gap, divide by SE and compare</h4>\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{\\max(0,|\\hat p_1-\\hat p_2|-c_Y)}{SE_0}\\]\\[D_Y=|.565-.455|-.0049609=.105039\\]\\[|z_Y|=\\frac{.105039}{.0498017}=2.10915>1.960\\]<p>Reject equal female-birth probabilities at .05; the father-exposed sample has the larger female proportion.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session11.pdf"],"topics":["rounded-proportions","dioxin"],"provenance":["V5: actual HW2 rounded-proportion case, rewritten with V6 operation labels"],"page":4,"column":1},"bonferroni-pairs":{"title":"Bonferroni: remaining dose pairs","html":"\\[SE_{ij}=.580421,\\quad t_{\\rm crit}=2.49367\\]<div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>5. Low versus High</h4>\\[|t_{ij}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_i-\\bar x_j|}{SE_{ij}}\\]\\[|t_{LH}|=\\frac{|3.7-5.2|}{.580421}=2.58433\\]\\[2.58433>2.49367\\Rightarrow\\text{reject }H_{0,LH}\\]</div><div class=\"v7-step\"><h4>6. Medium versus High</h4>\\[|t_{ij}|=\\frac{|\\bar x_i-\\bar x_j|}{SE_{ij}}\\]\\[|t_{MH}|=\\frac{|4.6-5.2|}{.580421}=1.03373\\]\\[1.03373<2.49367\\Rightarrow\\text{do not reject }H_{0,MH}\\]<p><b>Conclusion:</b> only low versus high is supported as different at family .05. A nonsignificant pair does not establish that its population means are identical.</p></div>","sources":["hw2.pdf","session9.pdf"],"topics":["all-pairs","pairwise-equations"],"provenance":["V6: complete Low–High and Medium–High substitutions, adjacent-column continuation"],"page":3,"column":3},"chi-output":{"title":"Read the aspirin/placebo program output","html":"<pre>Pearson&#x27;s Chi-squared test with\nYates&#x27; continuity correction\nX-squared = 5.5772, df = 1\np-value = 0.0182</pre><p>This is the equivalent corrected chi-square for the same aspirin/placebo data: \\(\\chi_Y^2=z_Y^2\\). Read X-squared as the statistic and p-value as the upper-tail probability; .0182 &lt; .05 rejects equal event probabilities.</p>","sources":["session11.pdf","session12.pdf","session15.pdf"],"topics":["chi-square-output","z-chi-square-equivalence"],"provenance":["V5: authentic output, attached to the same clinical data as the risk example"],"page":4,"column":3}}};
